# Block keys are DOT-separated, not underscored: HalpLibe's BlockBuilder.build(name, id, logic)
# turns "amethyst_ore" into the translation key `betteroplenty.amethyst.ore` and keeps the
# underscored form only as the namespace id. BTA's own blocks read the same way (tile.log.oak.name).
# An underscored key here localises nothing and shows the raw string in game -- com.betteroplenty
# .BOPLang checks every registered block and item at client start and names anything missing.
# Item keys are NOT rewritten: ItemBuilder keeps them as given.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DESCRIPTIONS FOLLOW BTA'S SCHEME WHERE BTA HAS ONE. Rule 6 applied to voice: where BTA already
# says a thing a settled way, a ported block says it that way too, or it reads as an import.
# Swept 2026-07-27; PORTING-STANDARD.md section 7 is the authority, this is the index.
#
#   ore              A chunk of ruby embedded in stone.          <- tile.ore.diamond.stone.desc
#   storage block    A solid block of ruby.                      <- tile.block.olivine.desc
#   log              A log from a willow tree.                   <- tile.log.oak.desc
#   leaves           Leaves from a willow tree.                  <- tile.leaves.oak.desc
#   sapling          Plant to grow a willow tree.                <- tile.sapling.oak.desc
#   stone / cobble   A block of solid red rock. / Red rock,      <- tile.basalt.desc,
#                    smashed apart and reconstituted.               tile.cobble.basalt.desc
#   brick            Stacked bricks of red rock.                 <- tile.brick.clay.desc
#   fluid            Spring water that flows when placed.        <- tile.fluid.water.flowing.desc
#   bucket           A bucket full of steaming spring water.     <- item.bucket.iron.water.desc
#   tool             Pick some stone with this amethyst tool.    <- item.tool.pickaxe.iron.desc
#   armour           A sturdy amethyst helmet.                   <- item.armor.helmet.iron.desc
#
# A SECOND SENTENCE ONLY WHERE IT SAYS SOMETHING A PLAYER CANNOT OTHERWISE FIND OUT -- which BTA
# itself does 75 times in 745 -- never for decoration. In practice: which plank a log cuts into,
# which canopies take the biome tint, what standing in a fluid does to you, where a sapling refuses
# to root. Applying this cost some better-written lines; they said nothing a player could act on.
#
# TWO THINGS BTA NEVER DOES, so neither do we any more:
#   - It never names a tool tier in a description. Zero occurrences in 745. The ore lines used to
#     end "An iron pick will free it"; the gate is still enforced in code and recorded in the atlas,
#     which is where that belongs.
#   - It never counts out a crafting recipe. "Nine rubies, packed for storage" is now "A solid block
#     of ruby"; the recipe is in the recipe book.
#
# NOT CONVERTED, because BTA has no scheme to converge on -- its own are ad-hoc one-liners
# ("A beautiful yellow flower.", "A tasty red mushroom. Good for stew."): flowers, mushrooms,
# grasses, corals, the BOP soils and terrain blocks, and the gem items, which already sit in BTA's
# register. Do not flatten these into a fake scheme; there is nothing to match.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

