# Wood set descriptions.
#
# LOG DESCRIPTIONS FOLLOW BTA'S OWN FORM: "A log from a <species> tree." -- its wood sets are a
# matched family (tile.log.oak.desc "A log from an oak tree.", tile.leaves.oak.desc "Leaves from an
# oak tree.", tile.sapling.oak.desc "Plant to grow an oak tree."), and a BOP log that described
# itself differently read as another mod's block. Rule 6 applied to voice, same as the "<Species>
# Log" naming above. Each then adds which BTA plank it cuts into, because that mapping is the only
# way to find out in game; two-sentence descriptions are within BTA's own style, 75 of its 745 are.
#
# LEAVES FOLLOW THE MATCHING FORM: "Leaves from a <species> tree." Same reason, and the same rule
# for the second sentence -- it is there only where it says something a player cannot otherwise
# find out, never for decoration:
#
#   - the FOUR species whose leaves take the biome foliage tint say so (acacia, mangrove, redwood,
#     willow -- BlockBOPColorizedLeaves; palm and pine are in that upstream array too but are BTA's,
#     so they are not here). The others render their texture as painted and say nothing.
#   - persimmon says its fruit has yet to set, because that is a deliberate gap in the port.
#   - bamboo is not a tree, so it does not claim to be one; BTA has the same case in
#     tile.leaves.shrub.desc, "Leaves from a shrub." It also states its decay rule, which is its
#     own (BlockLogicLeavesBamboo looks for a stalk, not a log).
#   - apple states its fruit mechanic. BTA's own fruiting leaf reads the same way:
#     tile.leaves.cherry.flowering.desc, "Leaves from a cherry tree that are budding with fruit."
#
# This did cost some better-written lines ("A canopy thick enough to lose the sky in"). They said
# nothing a player could act on, and a block that describes itself in a voice BTA never uses reads
# as an import rather than as part of the game.
#
# SAPLINGS TAKE "Plant to grow a <species> tree." too, completing the family. This was held back at
# first on the grounds that several of ours carry a real condition BTA's never do -- mangrove wants
# sand or shallow water, redwood needs its eight neighbours clear, acacia refuses plain dirt. That
# was the wrong call and only looked right because the second-sentence rule had not been applied to
# them: BTA's form takes the first sentence and the condition takes the second, exactly as a log
# states its plank. Only those three say anything extra, plus the four species whose trunk is a
# vanilla log (maple, both autumns, persimmon) -- worth stating, because it is why they have no log
# block of their own and no wood to cut.
